# Splunk SIEM Alert Configurations
# ==================================
# SUPERSEDED. This file previously described six alerts as prose comments.
# It stated they were "documented as savedsearches.conf entries", but no
# stanzas were present — the content could be read, not deployed.
#
# Alerts are now GENERATED from the detection YAML in detections/ by
# contentctl. Running `contentctl build` produces a complete, installable
# Splunk app at dist/, including a real savedsearches.conf:
#
#   [BOTSv1 Excessive Failed Logons From Single Source - Rule]
#   action.correlationsearch.enabled = 1
#   action.risk = 1
#   action.risk.param._risk = [{"risk_object_field": "src",
#                               "risk_object_type": "system",
#                               "risk_score": 30}]
#   cron_schedule = 0 * * * *
#   dispatch.earliest_time = -70m@m
#   dispatch.latest_time = -10m@m
#   search = | tstats `security_content_summariesonly` count as failure_count ...
#
# Two things changed beyond the file format.
#
# First, these are correlation searches writing to the RISK index, not
# alerts writing to the notable index. Nothing reaches the analyst queue
# until accumulated entity risk crosses a threshold. See docs/rba-guide.md.
#
# Second, the searches themselves were rewritten to use tstats against CIM
# data models rather than raw index searches. The originals below are kept
# for comparison.
#
#
# ============================================================
# MIGRATION MAP — old alert to current detection
# ============================================================
#
#  Old alert                          Current detection                                Risk
#  ---------------------------------  -----------------------------------------------  ----
#  1. Brute-Force Detected            botsv1_excessive_failed_logons_from_single_...    30
#  2. Credential Compromise           botsv1_successful_logon_after_failed_logon_...    70
#  3. New Admin Account Created       botsv1_privileged_group_membership_change         40
#  4. SQL Injection Attempt           botsv1_web_sql_injection_attempt                  60
#  5. Large Outbound Data Transfer    (not migrated — see note below)                    —
#  6. Critical IDS Alert              botsv1_high_severity_suricata_ids_alert           45
#
# Alert 5 was not migrated. A 100 MB outbound transfer threshold produces
# almost nothing but false positives on any network carrying backups, cloud
# sync, or software distribution, and tuning it requires a baseline this
# project does not have. Shipping it as a risk contribution would have
# inflated entity scores with noise. The hunting query is retained in
# detection_queries.spl, where an analyst runs it deliberately rather than
# it firing on a schedule.
#
# Alert 3 changed scope during migration. "New Admin Account Created"
# watched EventCode 4720 (account creation). The current detection watches
# group membership modification via the Change data model, which is the
# event that actually confers privilege — creating an account grants
# nothing until it is added to a group.
#
#
# ============================================================
# DEPLOYING
# ============================================================
#
#   pip install contentctl
#   contentctl validate          # schema, references, RBA structure
#   contentctl build             # -> dist/botsv1_soc_detections-latest.tar.gz
#
# Install the resulting app on a search head running Splunk Enterprise
# Security. Both commands run in CI on every push — see
# .github/workflows/validate-content.yml.
